CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose) The purpose of this Act is to establish the Korea Credit Guarantee Fund, thereby contributing to the balanced development of the national economy by facilitating corporate financing through the provision of debt guarantees to enterprises with insufficient collateral and the securitization of corporate bonds, etc., while simultaneously establishing a sound credit order through the efficient management and operation of credit information. <Amended on Apr. 1, 2025>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 2 (Definitions) The terms used in this Act are defined as follows: <Amended on May 28, 2013; Dec. 31, 2018; Dec. 29, 2020; Apr. 1, 2025>
  1. The term "enterprise" means an individual or juristic person who runs the business, and an organization formed by such individual or juristic persons;
  2. The term "credit guarantee" means a guarantee provided by Korea Credit Guarantee Fund (hereinafter referred to as the "Fund") for any of the following obligations borne by enterprises:
  a. Monetary obligations borne by an enterprise to financial companies, etc. by receiving a loan of fund or other benefits, etc. from such financial companies, etc.;
  b. Monetary obligations under which an enterprise shall comply with a claim for indemnity in compensation for discharging guaranteed obligations by a financial company, etc. if such financial company, etc. guarantees the enterprise's obligations;
  c. Debentures raised by an enterprise under Article 119 of the Financial Investment Services and Capital Markets Act;
  d. Other monetary obligations prescribed by Presidential Decree, among the obligations of enterprises;
  3. The term "financial company, etc." means any of the following institutions:
  a. A bank under Article 2(1)2 of the Banking Act;
  b. The Korea Development Bank under the Korea Development Bank Act;
  c. The Industrial Bank of Korea under the Industrial Bank of Korea Act;
  d. The Export-Import Bank of Korea under the Export-Import Bank of Korea Act;
  e. A trust business entity under the Financial Investment Services and Capital Markets Act;
  f. The NongHyup Bank under Article 161-11 of the Agricultural Cooperatives Act;
  g. The Suhyup Bank under Article 141-4 of the Fisheries Cooperatives Act;
  h. Others prescribed by Presidential Decree, who run the business of financing enterprises;
  4. The term "creditor" means a creditor of obligations, for which the Fund provides a credit guarantee;
  5. The term "capital fund" means property created by the Fund as its financial base by means of contributions or other ways to achieve the purpose of this Act;
  6. The term "credit information" means the credit information under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Credit Information Use and Protection Act;
  7. The term "counter-guarantee" means a guarantee by the Fund to indemnify an original guarantor up to the amount of obligations discharged by the original guarantor under a guarantee referred to in Article 23-2(1) (hereinafter referred to as "original guarantee");
  7-2. The term "securitization of corporate bonds, etc." means asset-backed securitization under the Asset-Backed Securitization Act, which falls under any of the following items:
  a. A series of acts in which a special-purpose company under the Asset-Backed Securitization Act (hereinafter referred to as "securitization company") issues asset-backed securities using corporate bonds, etc. acquired from an asset holder (referring to a person under Article 2(2) of the Asset-Backed Securitization Act; hereinafter the same shall apply) as securitization assets, and pays the principal and interest or dividends on such securities with proceeds from the management, operation, or disposition of those securitization assets, or with borrowed funds;
  b. A series of acts (hereinafter referred to as the "securitization trust for corporate bonds, etc.") in which the Fund acquires corporate bonds, etc. from an enterprise or purchases them from financial companies, etc., designates such corporate bonds, etc. as securitization assets, establishes a trust for which the Fund serves as trustee, issues asset-backed securities based on the entrusted securitization assets, and pays the proceeds on such securities with proceeds from the management, operation, or disposition of those securitization assets, or with borrowed funds;
  8. The term "securitization guarantee" means that the Fund, in connection with the securitization of corporate bonds, etc., guarantees the obligations borne by a securitization company and the trustee of a securitization trust for corporate bonds, etc. under Article 23-3(1) (hereinafter referred to as the "securitization company, etc.");
  9. The term "guarantee-aligned investment" means that the Fund invests in an enterprise pursuant to Article 23-4(1).
  10. The term "corporate bonds, etc." means corporate bonds issued by an enterprise (including convertible bonds and bonds with warrants), loan claims against an enterprise, or property rights prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  11. The term "asset-backed securities" means corporate bonds issued by a securitization company under subparagraph 7-2a (hereinafter referred to as "asset-backed bonds"), and the beneficiary certificates issued by the Fund under subparagraph 7-2b (hereinafter referred to as "asset-backed certificates").
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 3 (Guarantee by priority) (1) The Fund shall provide credit guarantees by priority to small and medium enterprises which lack security solvency and funds corresponding to objectives prescribed by Presidential Decree, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) The term "small and medium enterprises" in paragraph (1) means small and medium enterprises defined in Article 2 of the Framework Act on Small and Medium Enterprises.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 4 (Juristic personality) (1) The Fund shall be a corporation.
  (2) The Fund shall be operated, as prescribed by this Act, orders issued under this Act and the articles of incorporation of the Fund.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 5 (Head office, branches, sub-branches and agencies) (1) The Fund shall have its head office, as stipulated in the articles of incorporation. <Amended on Jan. 20, 2015>
  (2) The Fund may establish branches, sub-branches or agencies wherever necessary, as stipulated in the articles of incorporation.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 6 (Acquisition of capital fund) (1) The capital fund of the Fund shall consist of the following:
  1. Contributions from the Government;
  2. Contributions from financial companies, etc.;
  3. Contributions from enterprises;
  4. Contributions from persons other than those referred to in subparagraphs 1 through 3.
  (2) The budget of contributions from the Government under paragraph (1)1 shall be under the jurisdiction of the Ministry of SMEs and Startups. <Amended on Jul. 26, 2017>
  (3) Financial institutions shall contribute to the Fund sums of money according to the rate prescribed by Prime Ministerial Decree, within the extent not exceeding 3/1000 per annum for the money loaned (referring to "contribution rate"); provided, in the case of the Nonghyup Bank and the Suhyup Bank under subparagraph 3f and g of Article 2, the contribution rate may be otherwise prescribed by Prime Ministerial Decree. <Amended on Dec. 31, 2018>
  (4) The scope of loans, the methods and timing of making contributions, and other matters necessary for contributions under paragraph (3) shall be prescribed by Prime Ministerial Decree.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 7 (Articles of incorporation) (1) The following matters shall be stated in the articles of incorporation of the Fund:
  1. Objectives;
  2. Name;
  3. Matters pertaining to the head office, branches, sub-branches and agencies;
  4. Matters pertaining to the capital fund;
  5. Matters pertaining to the board of policy under Article 10;
  6. Matters pertaining to the board of directors;
  7. Matters pertaining to executive officers and employees;
  8. Matters pertaining to business and the execution thereof;
  9. Matters pertaining to accounting;
  10. Matters pertaining to the method of giving public announcement;
  11. Matters pertaining to the amendments to the articles of incorporation;
  12. Other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) Where the Fund intends to amend the articles of incorporation, it shall obtain authorization from the Financial Services Commission, following a resolution thereon by the board of policy under Article 10.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 8 (Registration) (1) The Fund shall be registered, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) The Fund shall be established at the time when it registers such establishment at the location of its head office.
  (3) Matters required to be registered under paragraph (1) shall not oppose to a third party until they are registered.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 9 (Prohibition of use of similar names) No person other than the Fund under this Act or other Acts shall use the "Korea Credit Guarantee Fund" or any other name similar thereto.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

CHAPTER II BOARD OF POLICY

Article 10 (Establishment of board of policy) (1) A board of policy shall be established under the Fund.
  (2) The board of policy shall formulate basic policies on the business operations of the Fund, as prescribed by this Act, orders issued under this Act, and the articles of incorporation of the Fund.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 11 (Composition of board of policy) (1) The board of policy shall be comprised of 12 members, from among the following: <Amended on Jul. 26, 2017; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. The Chairperson of the Fund;
  2. One person nominated by the Chairperson of the Financial Services Commission, from among the public officials belonging thereto;
  3. 1 person nominated by the Minister of Planning and Budget, from among the public officials thereof;
  4. One person nominated by the Minister of SMEs and Startups, from among the public officials belonging thereto;
  5. One person nominated by the Governor of the Bank of Korea under the Bank of Korea Act, from among the executive officers belonging thereto;
  6. One person nominated by the president of the Industrial Bank of Korea under the Industrial Bank of Korea Act, from among the executive officers belonging thereto;
  7. One person nominated by the head of a bank under Article 2(1)2 of the Bank Act, which is designated by the Financial Services Commission and is involved in a banking and financing business for the general public and small-sized enterprises, from among the executive officers belonging thereto;
  8. Three persons, from among the executive officers or executives of financial company, etc.;
  9. Two persons, from among the representatives of associations of enterprises.
  (2) Members referred to in paragraph (1)8 and 9 shall be nominated by the Financial Services Commission; provided, members referred to in subparagraph 9 shall be nominated by the Financial Services Commission through consultation with the Minister of Trade, Industry and Resources. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) The Chairperson of the Fund shall become the chairperson of the board of policy.
  (4) Members referred to in paragraph (1)1 and 5 through 8 may designate any executive officer or employee of the relevant institution as their deputy members to perform duties on behalf of them.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 12 (Term of office of members) The term of office of each member under Article 11(1)8 and 9 shall be two years; provided, the term of office of a supplementary member shall be the remaining term of his or her predecessor.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 13 (Operation of board of policy) Matters necessary for the operation of the board of policy shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

CHAPTER III EXECUTIVE OFFICERS AND EMPLOYEES

Article 14 (Executive officers) The Fund shall have, as its executive officers, one chairperson, one deputy president, not more than seven senior executive directors, and one auditor.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 15 (Duties of executive officers) (1) The Chairperson shall represent the Fund and preside over the business affairs of the Fund.
  (2) The deputy president shall assist the Chairperson and act on behalf of the Chairperson, if the Chairperson is unable to perform his or her duties due to unavoidable circumstances.
  (3) Directors shall assist the Chairperson and the deputy president, and share the business affairs of the Fund.
  (4) Where both the Chairperson and the deputy president are unable to perform their duties due to unavoidable circumstances, a director in the order pre-designated by the Chairperson shall act on behalf of the Chairperson.
  (5) The chief audit executive shall audit the business affairs and accounting of the Fund.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 15-2 (Board of directors) (1) A board of directors shall be established under the Fund.
  (2) The board of directors shall be comprised of the Chairperson the deputy president, and directors.
  (3) The board of directors shall pass a resolution on important matters pertaining to the business affairs of the Fund.
  (4) The Chairperson shall call meetings of the board of directors and serve as a Chairperson.
  (5) A majority of the members of the board of directors shall constitute a quorum, and resolutions shall require the concurring vote of at least a majority of those present.
  (6) The chief audit executive may attend and present his or her opinion at any meeting of the board of directors.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 16 (Appointment of executive officers) (1) The Chairperson and the chief audit executive shall be appointed by the Financial Services Commission.
  (2) The deputy president and directors shall be appointed by the Financial Services Commission upon the recommendation of the Chairperson of the Fund.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 17 (Term of office of executive officers) The term of office of an executive officer shall be three years.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 18 (Dismissal of executive officers) The Financial Services Commission shall dismiss an executive officer of the Fund, if he or she falls under any of the following cases:
  1. Where he or she violates this Act, orders issued under this Act, or the articles of incorporation of the Fund;
  2. Where he or she is adjudged guilty in a criminal case;
  3. Where he or she is declared bankrupt;
  4. Where he or she becomes unable to perform his or her duties due to his or her mental or physical disorder.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 19 (Obligation of prohibition of concurrent offices) (1) No executive officers of the Fund shall engage in any other business without permission of the Financial Services Commission.
  (2) No current or former executive officers and employees of the Fund shall disclose confidential information that they came to know in the course of their duties.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 20 Deleted. <May 19, 2011>

Article 21 (Appointment and dismissal of employees) The employees of the Fund shall be appointed or dismissed by the Chairperson of the Fund.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 22 (Appointment of representative) The Chairperson of the Fund may appoint a representative duly empowered to do all judicial or extrajudicial acts in connection with the business of the Fund, from among the deputy president, directors, and employees of the Fund.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

CHAPTER IV BUSINESS AFFAIRS

Article 23 (Business affairs) (1) In order to achieve the purposes of this Act, the Fund shall perform the following business affairs: <Amended on May 28, 2013; Dec. 31, 2021; Dec. 29, 2023; Apr. 1, 2025>
  1. Management of its capital fund;
  2. Credit guarantees;
  2-2. Guarantee-aligned investment;
  2-3. Exercise of rights over assets acquired under Article 23-3(4);
  2-4. Operation of factoring for small and medium-sized enterprises and middle-standing enterprise under Article 23-5(1);
  3. Management consulting;
  4. Credit investigations and comprehensive management of credit information;
  5. Exercise of the right to demand indemnity;
  6. Investigations and research of the credit guarantee system;
  7. Securitization guarantees;
  8. Securitization trusts for corporate bonds, etc.;
  9. Business affairs incidental to those under subparagraphs 1, 2, 2-2 through 2-4, and 3 through 8, as approved by the Financial Services Commission.
  (2) In addition to the business affairs under paragraph (1), the Fund may conduct re-guarantee business. <Amended on Apr. 1, 2025>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 23-2 (Counter-guarantee) (1) Where the Fund intends to provide a counter-guarantee, it shall enter into a contract with any credit guarantee foundation established under the Regional Credit Guarantee Foundation Act (hereinafter referred to as "original guarantor").
  (2) A contract under paragraph (1) shall stipulate matters pertaining to the ceiling amount, period, requirements, etc. of the counter-guarantee and the contract shall be executed in an umbrella agreement form counter-guaranteeing the original guarantee which meets the requirements of counter-guarantee, within the ceiling amount and the period of the counter-guarantee stipulated under the counter-guarantee contract.
  (3) The amount of counter-guarantee of the Fund shall be calculated by multiplying the amount of original guarantee by a rate prescribed by Presidential Decree (hereinafter referred to as "counter-guarantee rate").
  (4) Where an original guarantor has subrogated for the Fund, the amount of compensation to be paid by the Fund shall be calculated by multiplying the amount not recovered by exercising the right to demand indemnity, out of the amount of subrogated by the original guarantor, by the counter-guarantee rate.
  (5) If the original guarantor recovers the amount of subrogation by exercising the right to demand indemnity under the original guarantee, he or she shall return the amount calculated by multiplying such recovered amount by the counter-guarantee rate to the Fund.
  (6) A claim for discharging counter-guaranteed obligations shall be made at the expiration of a period prescribed by Presidential Decree from the date of subrogation by the original guarantor, and other matters necessary for making a claim for discharging the counter-guaranteed obligations shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (7) Other matters necessary for the relation between the Fund and the original guarantor in connection with the operation of counter-guarantee shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 23-3 (Securitization guarantees) (1) The Fund may provide guarantees for obligations incurred by a securitization company, etc. where such company, etc. uses corporate bonds, etc. as securitization assets and falls under any of the following subparagraphs: <Amended on Dec. 31, 2018; Apr. 1, 2025>
  1. Issuance of securitized securities;
  2. Granting credits to a special purpose company by financial companies, etc.
  (2) The maximum amount of securitization assets that an asset holder may acquire from the same enterprise, in connection with the operation of securitization guarantees by the Fund, shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Apr. 1, 2025>
  (3) The Fund may be entrusted the management of securitization assets or the performance of other business affairs from a securitization company, etc. that has obtained a securitization guarantee under Articles 10(1) and 23(1) of the Asset-Backed Securitization Act. <Amended on Apr. 1, 2025>
  (4) Where the Fund performs the guaranteed obligations for a securitization company, etc. that has obtained a securitization guarantee, it may acquire assets from such securitization company, etc. <Added on Apr. 1, 2025>
  (5) Where the Financial Services Commission deems it necessary for the efficient operation of the Fund and the appropriate diversification of risks, it may establish and notify separate guidelines. <Amended on Apr. 1, 2025>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]
  [Title Amended on Apr. 1, 2025]

Article 23-4 (Guarantee-aligned investment) (1) The Fund may invest in an enterprise with which it has established the relationship of a credit guarantee by any of the following methods: <Amended on Dec. 29, 2020>
  1. Acquisition of stocks;
  2. Underwriting convertible bonds;
  3. Underwriting bonds with warrants;
  4. Underwriting exchangeable bonds;
  5. Acquiring equity interest pursuant to Article 287-8 or 556 of the Commercial Act;
  6. Other methods prescribed by Presidential Decree that are equivalent to the methods referred to in subparagraphs 1 through 5.
  (2) A ceiling on the aggregate of guarantee-aligned investments by the Fund shall be determined by Presidential Decree, within the extent not exceeding 10/100 of the sum of capital fund of the Fund and the profits carried forward.
  (3) A ceiling on guarantee-aligned investments which the Fund may provide to the same enterprise shall be determined by Presidential Decree.
  (4) If deemed necessary for efficiently operating the Fund and appropriately diversifying risks, the Financial Services Commission may establish separate guidelines and give notice thereon.
  [This Article Added on May 28, 2013]

Article 23-5 (Operation of factoring for small and middle-standing enterprises) (1) In order to support the stable financing of any of the following persons (hereafter in this Article referred to as "small and medium entrepreneur, etc."), the Government may have the Credit Guarantee Fund perform the business of purchasing without the right to claim redemption trade receivables acquired through providing goods or services by small and medium enterprises, etc., thereby providing funds to small and medium entrepreneur, etc., and collecting the proceeds from the debtor on the maturity date of trade receivables (hereinafter referred to as "factoring for SMEs and middle-standing enterprises"). <Amended on Dec. 29, 2023>
  1. A small and medium entrepreneur defined in subparagraph 1a and b of article 2 of the Small and Medium Enterprises Promotion Act;
  2. A middle-standing business entrepreneur prescribed by Presidential Decree in consideration of the size of the enterprise, such as sales, from among the middle-standing business entrepreneurs defined in subparagraph 3 of Article 2 of the Special Act on the Promotion of Growth and the Strengthening of Competitiveness of Middle-Standing Enterprises;
  (2) Other necessary matters such as the scale, methods, procedures, etc., of operating the factoring for SMEs and middle-standing enterprises shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Dec. 29, 2023>
  [This Article Added on Dec. 31, 2021]
  [Title Amended on Dec. 29, 2023]

Article 23-6 (Securitization trusts of corporate bonds, etc.) (1) The Fund may acquire corporate bonds, etc., from enterprises or take them over from financial institutions, etc., under an asset securitization plan, and establish a trust with the Fund as the trustee based on those assets, and issue asset-backed certificates.
  (2) Notwithstanding Article 3(2) of the Trust Act, the establishment of a trust under paragraph (1) shall take effect from the time the asset-backed securitization plan is registered with the Financial Services Commission, and the fund may reserve the right to terminate the trust.
  (3) Where the Fund acquires, holds, or entrusts corporate bonds, etc. under paragraph (1), or issues asset-backed certificates on their basis, it shall be deemed to have obtained authorization under Article 12 of the Financial Investment Services and Capital Markets Act. In such cases, Articles 30 through 33, 40, 61, and 63 (limited to officers and employees who do not perform the duties of financial investment business under Article 6 of that Act), and 103(3), subparagraph 6 of Article 108, Articles 114, 415 through 418, 420, and 422 of that Act shall not apply.
  (4) Where the Fund engages in a securitization trust for corporate bonds, etc. based on loan claims, the Fund shall, with respect to such business, be regarded as a credit financial institution under subparagraph 4 of Article 2 of the Act on Registration of Credit Business and Protection of Finance Users.
  (5) Notwithstanding Article 111 of the Financial Investment Services and Capital Markets Act and Article 36 of the Trust Act, the Fund may acquire asset-backed certificates backed by assets other than corporate bonds, etc., as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (6) Where necessary to terminate a trust established under paragraph (1), or where necessary for the performance of payment guarantee obligations for asset-backed certificates, the Fund may purchase corporate bonds, etc. constituting securitization assets, notwithstanding Article 34 of the Trust Act.
  (7) Where a trust established under paragraph (1) is terminated, the Fund shall, without delay, prepare a final account for the trust business and publicly disclose the final account, and matters relating to such disclosure shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. In such cases, notwithstanding Article 103(1) of the Trust Act, if the beneficiary or the person entitled to residual assets does not raise an objection within 1 month after the disclosure of the final account, they shall be deemed to have approved the final account.
  [This Article Added on Apr. 1, 2025]

Article 24 (Operations manual) The Fund shall prepare an operations manual stating the details of the following matters necessary for the performance of business and shall obtain a resolution of the Steering Committee. The same shall apply when a change is intended to be made in the manual. <Amended on May 28, 2013; Apr. 1, 2025>
  1. The following matters concerning credit guarantee:
  a. Methods of providing a guarantee;
  b. Types of business for which guarantee is restricted;
  c. Period of guarantee;
  d. Guarantee fees;
  e. Discharge of guaranteed obligations;
  2. Exercise of the right to demand indemnity;
  3. The following matters concerning counter-guarantee:
  a. Methods of providing a counter-guarantee;
  b. Period of counter-guarantee;
  c. Types of business for which counter-guarantee is restricted;
  4. The following matters regarding securitization guarantees:
  a. Obligations subject to guarantee;
  b. Assets subject to securitization;
  c. Risk management plans;
  d. Maximum ceiling of securitized assets underwritable from the same enterprise under Article 23-3(2);
  4-2. Matters concerning guarantee-aligned investment;
  4-3. The following matters regarding the securitization trust for corporate bonds, etc.:
  a. The types of corporate bonds, etc. to be placed in the trust;
  b. The methods for acquiring or purchasing corporate bonds, etc.;
  c. Plans for managing and operating the assets referred to in items a and b;
  d. The method of distributing proceeds from the asset-backed certificates;
  e. Borrowing of funds for the securitization trust for corporate bonds, etc.;
  5. Other matters necessary for performing business of the Fund.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 25 (Ceiling of guarantee) (1) The aggregate ceiling on the Fund's credit guarantees, re-guarantees, and securitization guarantees shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree within a limit not exceeding 20 times the total amount of the Fund's fundamental property and profits carried forward. <Amended on Apr. 1, 2025>
  (2) A maximum ceiling on a credit guarantee or counter-guarantee the Fund may provide to the same enterprise shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 26 (Business plan) (1) The Fund shall prepare a business plan for each business year and obtain approval therefor from the Financial Services Commission, following a resolution thereon by the board of policy.
  (2) Where the Fund intends to obtain approval under paragraph (1), it shall submit it to the Financial Services Commission no later than one month prior to the commencement of the relevant year.
  (3) Any amendment to business plans shall be approved by the Financial Services Commission, following a resolution thereon by the board of policy.
  (4) When the Fund prepares or amends a business plan, it shall expressly state matters concerning a guarantee by priority it intends to provide under Article 3.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 27 (Duties to conduct investigations) Where the Fund provides credit guarantees (including securitization guarantees; hereinafter the same shall apply) under Article 23(1)2, or conducts credit investigations under Article 23(1)4, it shall fairly and diligently examine the management status, business prospects, credit standing, and other relevant matters. <Amended on Apr. 1, 2025>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 28 (Establishment of guaranty relation) (1) Where the Fund decides to provide a credit guarantee to an enterprise, it shall notify the enterprise and the person who will become the enterprise's creditor of such decision; provided, where the Fund guarantees bonds under Article 2(2)d, or obligations incurred by a securitization company, etc. upon issuing asset-backed securities under Article 23-3(1)1, it shall not be required to give notice to the underwriter of the bonds or the purchasers of the asset-backed securities. <Amended on Apr. 1, 2025>
  (2) The relationship of a credit guarantee shall take effect only when an agreement on primary obligations comes into effect between the relevant enterprise and its creditors, following receipt of the notice under paragraph (1).
  (3) No relationship of a credit guarantee shall take effect, unless an agreement on primary obligations comes into effect within 60 days from the date on which the notice under paragraph (1) is given to interested parties.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 29 (Discharge of guaranteed obligations) (1) A creditor may request the Fund to discharge guaranteed obligations if grounds prescribed by Presidential Decree arise.
  (2) Upon receipt of a request for the discharge of guaranteed obligations under paragraph (1), the Fund shall discharge both primary obligations and subordinate obligations prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 30 (Exercise of right to demand indemnity) (1) Where the Fund has discharged its guaranteed obligations, the relevant creditor shall send all documents necessary for the Fund to exercise its right to demand indemnity to the Fund, and shall fully cooperate with the Fund in exercising such right to demand indemnity.
  (2) Where any enterprise for which the Fund has subrogated falls under any of the following cases, the Fund may suspend the exercise of the right to demand indemnity against such enterprise, following a resolution thereon by the board of directors:
  1. Where it is deemed improbable that the property of the enterprise will, after being appropriated for the expenses incidental to the exercise of right to demand indemnity, still leave a balance;
  2. Where it is deemed probable that the debt-servicing ability of the enterprise will be enhanced by the suspension of the exercise of right to demand indemnity.
  (3) Where the Fund has suspended the exercise of the right to demand indemnity under paragraph (2)2, it may dispatch its executive officers or employees to the relevant enterprise to participate in the management thereof.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 30-2 (Sale of indemnity claims) Where the Fund deems it necessary for the efficient recovery and management of its indemnity claims (including assets acquired from a securitization company, etc. under Article 23-3(4); hereinafter the same shall apply), it may sell such indemnity claims to any of the following persons, upon a resolution of the Board of Directors: <Amended on Nov. 26, 2019; Apr. 1, 2025>
  1. A corporate restructuring investment company referred to in the Corporate Restructuring Investment Companies Act;
  2. A corporate restructuring association registered pursuant to Article 15 of the Industrial Development Act (referring to the Industrial Development Act before being wholly amended by that Act (Act No. 9584));
  3. Korea Asset Management Corporation established in accordance with the Act on the Establishment of Korea Asset Management Corporation;
  4. A special purpose company established in accordance with the Asset-Backed Securitization Act;
  5. Other persons prescribed by Presidential Decree who specialize in the purchase, sale and management of non-performing loans.
  [This Article Added on May 28, 2013]

Article 30-3 (Reduction of and exemption from jointly guaranteed obligations) Where a creditor is the Fund, if the primary obligations are reduced or exempt at the time approval for rehabilitation plans for a small and medium enterprise is obtained or immunity is granted after adjudication of bankruptcy, jointly guaranteed obligations shall also be reduced or exempt at the same rate, notwithstanding Articles 250(2), 567 and 625(3) of the Debtor Rehabilitation and Bankruptcy Act.
  [This Article Added on May 28, 2013]

Article 31 (Creditors' duties to give notice) In any of the following cases, a creditor prescribed by Presidential Decree shall notify the Fund of such fact without delay:
  1. Where an agreement on primary obligations has come into effect;
  2. Where all or some of the obligations involved have been extinguished;
  3. Where a debtor has forfeited the benefit of the term;
  4. Where a debtor has failed to discharge his or her obligation;
  5. Where other grounds which may affect the guaranteed obligations have occurred.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 31-2 (Delegation provisions concerning comprehensive management of credit information) Matters necessary for affairs such as comprehensive management of credit information, credit analysis and evaluations, etc. shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 32 (Entrustment of business) (1) The Fund may entrust part of its business to financial companies, the Korea Asset Management Corporation under the Act on the Establishment of Korea Asset Management Corporation, or claims collection agencies under the Credit Information Use and Protection Act (hereafter in this Article referred to as "claims collection agencies"), as prescribed by Presidential Decree; provided, in the case of a debt collection company, the duties that may be entrusted shall be limited to those permitted under the Credit Information Use and Protection Act. <Amended on Nov. 26, 2019; Feb. 4, 2020>
  (2) Any person entrusted with the business under paragraph (1) may do all judicial or extrajudicial acts in connection with such business on behalf of the Fund; provided, claims collection agencies shall act on behalf of the Fund only in the extrajudicial area. <Amended on Feb. 4, 2020>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 33 (Guarantee fees) (1) The Fund shall collect guarantee fees from an enterprise that has received a credit guarantee (including a securitization company, etc. that has received a securitization guarantee; hereinafter the same shall apply), taking into account the enterprise's credit standing and other relevant factors, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. In such cases, where an enterprise that has received a credit guarantee achieves remarkable management performance, the Fund may receive a performance-based guarantee fee from the enterprise under a separate agreement, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Apr. 1, 2025>
  (2) The Fund shall collect a counter-guarantee fee for the amount of counter-guarantee from persons provided with a counter-guarantee, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (3) When an enterprise provided with a credit guarantee fails to pay the guarantee fee by due date, the Fund shall collect an overdue guarantee fee for the unpaid guarantee fee, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 33-2 (Fees) The Fund may charge fees when performing the business affairs under Article 23(1) 3, 4, 8, and 9, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Apr. 1, 2025>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 34 (Penalties) Where the Fund is not relieved from its responsibility to discharge guaranteed obligations since an enterprise provided with a guarantee fails to discharge its obligations by the due date, the Fund is entitled to collect a penalty from the relevant enterprise for the amount in default out of the guaranteed obligations, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 35 (Damages) Where the Fund discharges a guaranteed obligation, it shall collect damages from the relevant enterprise at a rate not exceeding 25/100 per annum of the amount paid by the Fund.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

CHAPTER V ACCOUNTING

Article 36 (Accounting Principles) (1) The accounting of the Fund shall be in accordance with business accounting standards.
  (2) The capital fund of the Fund shall be assessed as capital in the accounting.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 37 (Fiscal year) The fiscal year of the Fund shall coincide the fiscal year of the Government.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 38 (Budget) (1) The Fund shall compile a budget indicating the total income and expenditure of each business year and it shall obtain approval therefor from the Financial Services Commission, following a resolution by the board of policy.
  (2) Where the Fund intends to obtain approval under paragraph (1), it shall submit the budget bill to the Financial Services Commission no later than one month prior to the commencement of the relevant fiscal year.
  (3) Where the Fund intends to revise its budget, it shall obtain approval therefor from the Financial Services Commission, following a resolution by the board of policy.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 39 (Settlement of accounts) The Fund shall prepare a report on the statement of accounts, a statement of financial position, a profit and loss statement and a statement of capital fund for each fiscal year and submit them to the Financial Services Commission within two months after the end of the relevant year, following a resolution thereon by the board of policy. <Amended on Apr. 20, 2021>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 40 (Management of capital fund) (1) The capital fund of the Fund shall be appropriated for the expenditure incurred in the business operations of the Fund and the reserve funds shall be managed in any of the following methods; provided, in cases under subparagraphs 3 and 4, approval of the Financial Services Commission shall be obtained:
  1. Deposit with financial companies, etc.;
  2. Purchase of state bonds, local government bonds, and bonds, the payment of which is guaranteed by the Government, a local government, financial companies, etc.;
  3. Underwriting or purchase of stocks (including investment certificates), debentures or other securities;
  4. Other methods necessary for the achievement of the objective, for which the Fund is established.
  (2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), the Fund may contribute part of its capital fund to the Korea SMEs and Startups Agency established in accordance with the Small and Medium Enterprises Promotion Act. In such cases, the Fund shall consult with the Minister of Planning and Budget thereabout and obtain approval therefor from the Financial Services Commission. <Amended on Dec. 31, 2018; Oct. 1, 2025>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 41 (Coverage of losses) (1) Where any profit is realized in the settlement of accounts of the Fund, the total amount of such profit shall be reserved.
  (2) Where any loss is incurred in the settlement of accounts of the Fund, it shall be covered by the reserves under paragraph (1), and if the relevant reserves are insufficient to cover such loss, the Government shall cover it.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

CHAPTER VI SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 42 (Supervision) The Financial Services Commission may supervise the business of the Fund and issue orders necessary for supervising the Fund.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 43 (Reporting and inspections) (1) If it is deemed necessary to achieve the purposes of this Act, the Financial Services Commission may request the Fund, financial companies, etc. entrusted by the Fund with the business thereof (hereinafter referred to as "trustee") or financial companies, etc. making contributions to the Fund to submit reports, or cause public officials affiliated therewith to inspect the status of operations, books and documents or other necessary matters; provided, in cases of a trustee, such reporting and inspection shall be limited to the entrusted matters, and in cases of financial companies, etc. making contributions to the Fund, such reporting and inspection shall be limited to contribution-related matters.
  (2) The Financial Services Commission may entrust the Governor of the Financial Supervisory Service under Article 24 of the Act on the Establishment of Financial Services Commission with the inspections under paragraph (1).
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 43-2 (Request for provision of data and cooperation) (1) The Fund may request the provision of data necessary for performing the business affairs under Article 23(1)2 and 2-2 through 2-4, 3 through 9, and Article 23-3(4) from the State, local governments, the National Pension Service under the National Pension Act, the National Health Insurance Service under the National Health Insurance Act, the Korea Labor Welfare Corporation under the Industrial Accident Compensation Insurance Act, and other public entities prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Apr. 1, 2025>
  (2) The Chairperson of the Fund may request the head of the competent tax office or the competent local government to provide taxation information (limited to the details of taxation data on the global income tax, local tax, and other related business registration data) by submitting a written document stating the following matters; in such cases, the request for provision of taxation information shall be made to the minimum extent necessary to exercise rights over the assets acquired under Article 23(1)2-3, or for exercising the right of recourse under subparagraph 5 of that paragraph and shall not be abused for any other purpose: <Added on Jan. 14, 2014; Apr. 1, 2025>
  1. The relevant taxpayer's personal information;
  2. Purpose of the use of information.
  (3) The Fund may request cooperation from enterprises to efficiently perform the comprehensive management of credit information under Article 23(1)4. <Amended on Jan. 14, 2014>
  (4) Any person in receipt of a request for data or cooperation under paragraphs (1) through (3) shall comply therewith unless there is a compelling reason not to do so. <Amended on Jan. 14, 2014>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 44 (Liability of compensation) (1) Where any executive officer of the Fund violates statutes or the articles of incorporation of the Fund or neglects his or her duties, he or she shall be jointly and severally liable to the Fund for any loss resulting therefrom.
  (2) Where any person engaged in the credit guarantee business of the Fund, incurs any loss to the Fund, by intent or gross negligence, in connection with his or her duties, he or she shall be liable for compensation for such loss. In such cases, such liability may be mitigated, except in cases where a loss is intentionally incurred.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

Article 45 Deleted. <Aug. 4, 1995>

Article 46 (Prohibition of guarantee) The Fund may prohibit any enterprise from obtaining a guarantee in accordance with the operations manual under Article 24, if it damages the soundness of the Fund by evading its obligations by improper means or is operated or represented by such enterprise's directors or managing general partners.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 29, 2016]

Article 47 Deleted. <Dec. 31, 2001>

Article 48 Deleted. <May 19, 2011>

Article 48-2 (Legal fiction as public officials for purposes of applying penalty provisions) Any executive officer of the Fund shall be deemed a public official for purposes of applying penalty provisions under the Criminal Act or any other Act.
  [This Article Added on May 19, 2011]

CHAPTER VII PENALTY PROVISIONS

Article 49 (Penalty provisions) (1) Any person who discloses confidential information in violation of Article 19(2) shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than two years or by a fine not exceeding 20 million won. <Amended on Nov. 26, 2019>
  (2) Any person who uses the Korea Credit Guarantee Fund or any other name similar thereto in violation of Article 9 shall be punished by a fine not exceeding five million won.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 19, 2011]

